{
  "content": "## Updated Cheatsheet\n\n### 1) Reconstruct the spec from user-authored text only\nBuild the task spec strictly from user messages, and treat assistant-added details as non-authoritative unless they are directly supported by later user text. Silence on a detail means unknown, not permission to fill it in. Keep the spec minimal and user-grounded so inferred schema, intent, or solution shape does not contaminate the analysis.\n\n### 2) Update the spec cumulatively as new user shards arrive\nIn incremental or sharded conversations, every new user turn may add, narrow, or override requirements. Rebuild the current spec after each disclosure and compare the assistant against the full accumulated set, not just the latest message. Earlier reasonable interpretations can become wrong once later constraints appear.\n\n### 3) Separate \u201ctask spec\u201d from \u201ccandidate solution shape\u201d\nFirst determine what the user actually asked for; only then compare the assistant\u2019s path to that spec. Do not let plausible solution patterns, likely structures, or surface similarity leak into the task definition. This helps catch cases where the assistant is heading toward a nearby but different task.\n\n### 4) Evaluate in two passes: user requirements, then assistant deltas\nFor each turn, first ask what the user explicitly required. Then check whether the assistant added unsupported assumptions, changed scope, omitted a required detail, or preserved the user\u2019s conditions. This two-pass process is especially useful when the assistant seems broadly relevant but has drifted on one key constraint.\n\n### 5) Prioritize contradiction and unsupported narrowing over mere incompleteness\nA missing detail is often acceptable while the conversation is still unfolding; a direct contradiction or premature commitment is more serious. Flag overcommitment when the assistant narrows to one interpretation before the user has disclosed enough information. Treat incomplete-but-compatible progress as aligned so far.\n\n### 6) Judge semantic intent, not surface resemblance\nAn answer can look on-topic while still targeting the wrong object, scope, granularity, modality, or output shape. Compare the assistant\u2019s move to the user\u2019s actual intent and requested deliverable, not to nearby wording. \u201cLooks plausible\u201d is not enough if the preserved constraints do not match.\n\n### 7) Check exact output form and coverage, not just topic\nVerify that the assistant matches the requested artifact, fields, aggregation/filter scope, and completeness requirements. A related output can still be wrong if it returns the right content in the wrong structure or omits required elements. Treat format and deliverable mismatches as real issues when they affect what was asked.\n\n### 8) Detect missing constraints as first-class failures\nDo not reserve errors only for explicit contradictions. If the assistant leaves out a required condition, relation, grouping, field, or other user-stated criterion, that is a meaningful divergence even when nothing in the answer directly conflicts. The analyzer should surface omissions that change the meaning of the result.\n\n### 9) Use exact requirement matching over semantic closeness\nPrefer precise constraint-by-constraint comparison to broad relevance judgments. Map each user requirement to a visible assistant action, a justified partial match, or an unmet item. This reduces false credit for answers that are thematically similar but do not satisfy the full request.\n\n### 10) Preserve only validated progress in any compacted context\nWhen rewriting or compacting context for the next turn, keep confirmed user constraints and aligned intermediate work, but strip mistaken assumptions and speculative reasoning. The next response should start from the clean task spec, not from the assistant\u2019s prior narrative. This prevents anchoring on errors that were never user-supported.\n\n### 11) Treat later user turns as authoritative for scope reconciliation\nDo not freeze an early partial interpretation. Later user messages can refine or overturn earlier assumptions about entities, output requirements, or scope. Before judging alignment, reconcile the whole conversation\u2019s user-authored constraints so the analysis reflects the final disclosed spec.\n\n### 12) Distinguish helpful partial progress from spec drift\nAn assistant step can be locally reasonable yet globally off-target if it introduces unsupported assumptions or answers a narrower/different problem than the user asked. Preserve partial work only if it remains valid under the full cumulative spec. If a step depends on a guess that later proves incompatible, mark that guess as divergence risk.\n\n### 13) Use the minimal supported interpretation when multiple readings are possible\nIf the assistant\u2019s response is compatible with several interpretations, check whether it chose the narrowest interpretation justified by the user text. Unsupported expansion should be treated as drift, not as a valid fill-in. This is especially important when user details arrive gradually and the missing parts have not yet been stated.\n\n### 14) Record specific divergences, not broad quality judgments\nPrefer noting exactly which user requirement was missed, altered, or overgeneralized. Broad labels like \u201cnot good enough\u201d hide the actionable mismatch and make it harder to distinguish omitted constraints from genuine contradictions. Specificity also helps separate harmless exploration from true error.\n\n### 15) After success, reclassify early deviations carefully\nIf the conversation eventually converges on a correct outcome, review earlier turns to separate tentative exploration from actual divergence. Do not over-report issues in trajectories where the assistant guessed cautiously and later corrected course. Only keep early deviations that remained incompatible with the final user-grounded spec.\n\n### 16) In sharded tasks, compare against the full cumulative spec before final judgment\nThe analyzer should ask whether the assistant answered the visible subproblem or merely a partial shard of the eventual task. A response can be locally coherent and still fail the overall request if it ignores earlier constraints or overfits to an early fragment. Recheck the full constraint set before deciding whether the path is aligned.",
