    "",
    "## Updated Cheatsheet\n\n### 1) Build the task spec only from user-authored content\nStart by extracting a minimal spec from explicit user statements only. Keep user-grounded constraints separate from anything the assistant inferred, guessed, or optimistically filled in. Treat unsupported specifics as unknowns, not as implied requirements. This prevents the analyzer from baking in assumptions that later hide the real divergence.\n\n### 2) Treat each new user turn as an authoritative constraint update\nWhen the conversation is sharded or incremental, every new user message may add, narrow, or override constraints. Merge the new shard into a running spec and re-check earlier interpretations against the updated requirement set. Do not freeze the first reading; the analyzer should continually revise its understanding as the user reveals more.\n\n### 3) Compare the assistant against the evolving spec after every turn\nDo not wait until the end to decide whether the assistant diverged. After each turn, check whether the current assistant path still satisfies all disclosed constraints so far. This catches early misframing, wrong entities, and premature narrowing before they compound into later mistakes.\n\n### 4) Use compatibility checking, not completion checking\nJudge whether the assistant\u2019s current approach can still satisfy the full accumulated spec, not whether it has already solved everything. In-progress work can be incomplete and still aligned. The higher-priority issue is a direct contradiction, a wrong target, or a chosen path that has become incompatible with the user\u2019s disclosed requirements.\n\n### 5) Flag overcommitment early when the assistant narrows too soon\nIf the assistant commits to a concrete direction before the user has revealed all needed constraints, treat that as a likely anchoring error. A response can sound reasonable while still being narrower than the user\u2019s request. The analysis should surface the risky assumption as soon as it appears, because later turns may be forced to work around the mistaken framing.\n\n### 6) Distinguish aligned partial progress from actual divergence\nDo not penalize the assistant simply for not solving unrevealed parts of the task. If the assistant\u2019s work is directionally correct and does not yet conflict with any user constraint, label it as aligned so far. Reserve error flags for omissions that matter to the stated goal or for moves that directly violate the accumulated spec.\n\n### 7) Check semantic alignment, not surface resemblance\nAn answer can look relevant while still targeting the wrong object, scope, granularity, modality, or field set. Compare the assistant\u2019s action to the user\u2019s actual intent and requested artifact, not just to nearby wording. \u201cLooks right\u201d should only count if the preserved constraints match, not if the phrasing sounds on-topic.\n\n### 8) Use a contradiction-first pass before giving credit\nFirst ask whether any assistant move directly conflicts with the current user spec. Only after ruling out conflict should you assess whether the remaining work is reusable or merely incomplete. This reduces false positives from sparse but successful trajectories and keeps the analyzer focused on genuine divergence.\n\n### 9) Verify the exact requested output form and deliverable\nCheck the final response against the user\u2019s requested artifact, fields, and modality, not just the underlying topic or intent. A semantically related output can still be wrong if it misses required structure, target entity, or completeness. Format mismatches and deliverable-type mismatches should be treated as real issues when they affect what the user asked for.\n\n### 10) Keep inferred details provisional until user-supported\nAny assistant-added schema choice, relation, field, operator, or other concrete detail should remain provisional unless later user text explicitly supports it. The analyzer should distinguish \u201cuser asked for\u201d from \u201cassistant inferred,\u201d and avoid letting provisional details contaminate the task spec. This is especially important in structured tasks where a plausible choice can still be the wrong one.\n\n### 11) In sharded conversations, judge against all disclosed constraints so far\nDo not evaluate each shard in isolation. Carry forward earlier user constraints, merge them with the latest turn, and check whether the assistant remains compatible with the full set. A mismatch may reflect failure to integrate earlier shards, not just a mistake in the most recent exchange.\n\n### 12) Separate incomplete from contradictory; prioritize contradictions\nMissing information is often acceptable while the conversation is still unfolding. Directly violating a stated requirement, using the wrong target, or committing to an incompatible framing is the more serious issue. The analyzer should surface contradictions first and treat mere incompleteness as lower priority unless it caused overcommitment.\n\n### 13) For context edits, preserve only validated progress and user constraints\nWhen producing a compacted or rewritten context, keep the clean spec plus aligned intermediate work, but remove mistaken assumptions and misleading reasoning. The next turn should start from the true user-grounded state, not from the assistant\u2019s prior narrative. Do not strip away confirmed progress just because earlier reasoning contained an error.\n\n### 14) After a correct final outcome, distinguish tentative exploration from true error\nIf the conversation eventually succeeds, review earlier turns to identify which deviations were merely exploratory and which were actual divergences. This prevents over-reporting issues in trajectories where the assistant initially guessed cautiously but later corrected course. Successful sparse trajectories should not be penalized for lacking unnecessary detail.\n\n### 15) Prefer constraint-by-constraint evaluation\nMap each user requirement to a visible assistant action, a justified partial match, or an unmet item. This makes omissions and wrong commitments easier to localize and reduces the chance that a broad \u201coverall relevance\u201d judgment hides a specific missed constraint."
